program AgentsExe;

// WHERE SOMEBODY IS GOING, AND THE NEXT PLACE ON THE WAY THERE.
//
// NOT `Whereabouts`, WHICH IS WHERE THEY ARE, and not the activity, which is what they are doing --
// a person fetching water and a person walking home are doing different things toward the same kind
// of journey, and a person standing still has an activity and no journey at all. The three are
// separate because they change on different clocks: whereabouts every tick, a journey when a
// destination is reached or abandoned, an activity when the world gives them something else to do.
//
// TWO POINTS AND NOT A PATH, and that is the layout design's rule holding: a path is a list whose
// length nobody can predict, and a list per agent is an allocator per agent -- eleven of them per
// person is precisely what the previous build could not afford. So an agent remembers only where it
// is ultimately going and the next waypoint on the way, and asks for the next one when it arrives.
// The search that answers is bounded and rare; the memory is two vectors and a flag, forever.
public bundle Agents {
    public namespace People {

        public struct Journey {
            public mutable vec2 to;        // where it is ultimately going
            public mutable vec2 waypoint;  // the next place on the way, from the route search
            public mutable boolean going;  // false when it has arrived, or never set out
            // How many ticks running the ground has refused its step. A walker that gets nowhere
            // for long enough gives up -- which is the whole of what the previous build's agents
            // never did, and the reason they span against walls until somebody watched them.
            public mutable int balked;
            // The earliest tick it will ask for a route again. A walker whose waypoint lands within
            // arm's reach -- because the way on is short, or because there is barely a way on at all
            // -- would otherwise ask on every single tick: measured at twenty-four thousand searches
            // for three hundred journeys. Thinking has a rate, like everything else in this world.
            public mutable long thinkAt;
            // AND WHAT IT IS CHASING, if the destination is a body rather than a place: the slot of
            // the beast, or `NOTHING`. A hunt is a journey whose end keeps moving, which is why it
            // lives here and not in a part of its own -- everything else about it is a journey.
            //
            // (The work layout puts a `chase` on `Employment`, and that is the same field one slice
            // later: there a hunter is a POST, with a quarry the post assigns him. Today hunting is
            // an activity anybody hungry may enter, so the target belongs where the walking is. When
            // S4 gives the world its posts, the two meet here.)
            public mutable int chase;

            // Chasing nobody. A slot is an index, so nought is a real beast and the sentinel cannot
            // be it -- the same trap `Whereabouts` fell into and the whole world went to work at
            // building nought (ledger S2-15).
            public static fixed int NOTHING = 0 - 1;

            // How near counts as arrived, in cells. Half a cell: closer than that and a body is
            // standing on the spot, and asking for more is asking a walker to hit a point exactly,
            // which is how a thing ends up circling its own destination forever.
            public static fixed float CLOSE_ENOUGH = 0.5;

            // GOING NOWHERE AND CHASING NOTHING, which a zeroed struct does not say: `going` is
            // false, which is right, but `chase` is nought, which is the first beast in the world.
            // Nothing reads it while `going` is false -- and "nothing reads it yet" is exactly the
            // shape that has cost this slice five defects, so it is written rather than reasoned
            // about. Same as `Whereabouts.born` and `Body.born`.
            public static method born() returns Journey {
                mutable Journey fresh = new Journey();
                fresh.chase = Journey.NOTHING;
                return fresh;
            }
        }
    }
}
